ed a system of ethnic quotas so stringent that it choked off large-scale immigration for decades, sharply curtailing immigration from southern and eastern Europe and outright banning people from nearly all of Asia. In a compelling narrative with a fascinating cast of characters, Jia Lynn Yang recounts how a small number of lawmakers, activists, and presidents worked relentlessly for the next forty years to abolish the 1924 law and its quotas. Their efforts established the new mythology of the United States as \"a nation of immigrants\" that is so familiar to all of us now. Through a world war, a global refugee crisis, and a McCarthyist fever that swept the country, these Americans never stopped trying to restore the United States to a country that lived up to its vision as a home for \"the huddled masses\" from Emma Lazarus's famous poem.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Emigration and immigration law","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-one-mighty-and-irresistible-tide-the-epic-struggle-over-american-immigration-1924-1965-jia-lynn-"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Immigrants, united states","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-one-mighty-and-irresistible-tide-the-epic-struggle-over-american-immigration-1924-1965-jia-lynn-"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"United states, emigration and immigration","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-one-mighty-and-irresistible-tide-the-epic-struggle-over-american-immigration-1924-1965-jia-lynn-"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"United states, history, 20th century","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-one-mighty-and-irresistible-tide-the-epic-struggle-over-american-immigration-1924-1965-jia-lynn-"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-one-mighty-and-irresistible-tide-the-epic-struggle-over-american-immigration-1924-1965-jia-lynn-"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Immigrants","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-one-mighty-and-irresistible-tide-the-epic-struggle-over-american-immigration-1924-1965-jia-lynn-"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Emigration and immigration","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-one-mighty-and-irresistible-tide-the-epic-struggle-over-american-immigration-1924-1965-jia-lynn-"}],"displaySummary":"The idea of the United States as a nation of immigrants is today so pervasive, and seems so foundational, that it can be hard to believe Americans ever thought otherwise. But a 1924 law passed by Congress instituted a system of ethnic quotas so stringent that it choked off large-scale immigration for decades, sharply curtailing immigration from southern and eastern Europe and outright banning people from nearly all of Asia. In a compelling narrative with a fascinating cast of characters, Jia Lynn Yang recounts how a small number of lawmakers, activists, and presidents worked relentlessly for the next forty years to abolish the 1924 law and its quotas. Their efforts established the new mythology of the United States as \"a nation of immigrants\" that is so familiar to all of us now. Through a world war, a global refugee crisis, and a McCarthyist fever that swept the country, these Americans never stopped trying to restore the United States to a country that lived up to its vision as a home for \"the huddled masses\" from Emma Lazarus's famous poem.","primaryTopic":"Immigration, Refugees & Borderlands","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"American History","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"American History","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"American History","mappedSubject":"American History","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily explores the history of Amer